{
  "result": "I have been thinking lately about small joys, the kind that slip past us when we are busy chasing big milestones. This morning I watched sunlight pour through my kitchen window and turn dust motes into gold confetti. My coffee steamed, the cat stretched, and for a moment everything felt enough. It made me wonder why we postpone happiness until after some achievement, after graduation, promotion, move, or milestone, as if joy were a reward rather than a practice. Perhaps curiosity is the antidote. When I stay curious, ordinary things become portals: a laugh on the bus, the smell of rain on pavement, a library book with a stranger's notes in the margins. Each one reminds me that life is not just happening elsewhere, it is happening right here, quietly asking for attention. I want to get better at noticing. I want to ask more questions, take longer walks without headphones, learn names of trees, bake bread just to watch it rise, write letters by hand. There is so much world to taste, hear, and hold. Technology can connect us or scatter us, depending on how we use it. Lately I try to use it intentionally, to learn, to create, to reach out kindly, then put it down and look up. Wonder, I think, is a muscle. The more we exercise it, the stronger our gratitude grows, and the more generous we become with others. So here is my wish for today: slow down, notice more, and leave things better.",
